"comparison": "AVGO and MU both describe assessing long-lived assets (specifically property, plant, and equipment in MU's case) for impairment when carrying values may not be recoverable. AVGO triggers this assessment when events indicate non-recoverability, including negative trends. MU reviews carrying values for impairment 'when events and circumstances indicate that the carrying value... may not be recoverable' and explicitly uses an undiscounted cash flow test to determine if an impairment loss should be recognized if future cash flows are less than the carrying value. Both entities recognize impairment losses based on the difference between carrying value and fair value.",
